<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Boleslaus I of Bohemia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 967 or 972</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Boleslaus the Cruel, Boleslav I · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/premyslid-dynasty">Přemyslid dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Boleslaus I, called the Cruel, was Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> from 935, after he murdered his brother Wenceslas. He resisted Otto I for fourteen years before accepting his overlordship in 950, and his forces helped defeat the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lechfeld">Battle of Lechfeld</a> in 955. He extended Přemyslid power over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, Silesia and the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boleslaus-i-of-bohemia.jpg" alt="Boleslaus I of Bohemia" width="138" height="207" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Känsterle (Overleg) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boleslav-I-Bohemian.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Boleslaus was the son of Duke Vratislaus I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and Drahomíra, who was likely a Hevellian princess and was pagan. He and his elder brother Wenceslaus were taught the Roman Catholic faith and to read the Psalms by their grandmother Ludmila. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/premyslid-dynasty">Přemyslid dynasty</a> had ruled Bohemia since about 870, and Bořivoj I was its first duke to be baptised. After Vratislaus died, Wenceslaus succeeded him and kept his independence from the East Frankish ruler Henry I only by agreeing to pay annual tribute.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Wenceslaus was murdered at Stará Boleslav in 935, according to other sources in 929, after accepting Boleslaus's invitation to celebrate the feast of Saints Cosmas and Damian. Historians are uncertain of Boleslaus's motives, but argue that it was likely a desire for power, the influence of his pagan mother, or an end to Wenceslaus's political plans. Wenceslaus was soon venerated as a martyr and canonised, and some historians think Boleslaus promoted the cult because it benefited his rule.</p><p>Boleslaus stopped paying tribute, which led to war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I</a>. In 935 he attacked the Thuringian allies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> and defeated two of Otto's armies. The war declined into border raids until 950, when Otto besieged a castle held by Boleslaus's son Boleslaus II and Boleslaus agreed to peace, undefeated, and promised to resume tribute. Five years later Bohemian and Frankish armies allied against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lechfeld">Battle of Lechfeld</a>, and Boleslaus crushed the remainder of the Magyar army when it turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>.</p><p>He freed the Moravian lands from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyar</a> raids and expanded his territory to what later became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-silesia">Upper Silesia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesser-poland">Lesser Poland</a>. By occupying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> he controlled trade routes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lviv</a>. Prague was a major trading centre under him, and he had strongholds built across the countryside. About 963 or 964 he married his daughter Doubravka to the pagan Piast prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-i">Mieszko I</a>, who accepted baptism before the marriage. The alliance is often credited with the Roman Catholic conversion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Boleslaus was succeeded by his eldest son, Boleslaus II. Czech historians generally respect him as an energetic ruler who strengthened the Bohemian state and expanded its territory. Medieval writers, notably Cosmas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, present him as a cruel figure, and the date of his death given by Cosmas is questioned by modern researchers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Boleslaus I of Bohemia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Wenceslas, murdered by his brother <b>Boleslaus I</b> in 935, became the country's patron saint.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-bohemia">Duchy of Bohemia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/czech-republic">the Czech Republic</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/czech-republic">the Czech Republic</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.83,14.33&amp;year=962" aria-label="Bohemia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4879 429 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Boleslaus I of Bohemia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5329 699h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5329 699h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.83,14.33&amp;year=962">What was here in 962 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
